## Module filename suffixes (kebab-case + role)

Use **kebab-case** for the base name, then a **dot-suffix** before the extension that states the module’s primary role:

| Role | Pattern | Example |
|------|---------|---------|
| Interface module | `name-name.interface.ts` | `user-profile.interface.ts` |
| React component | `name-name.component.tsx` | `primary-button.component.tsx` |
| Type module (`type` aliases, unions, utilities) | `name-name.type.ts` | `api-error.type.ts` |
| Enum module | `name-name.enum.ts` | `order-status.enum.ts` |
| Constants | `name-name.const.ts` | `routes.const.ts` |
| Pure helpers / utilities (non-hook functions) | `name-name.util.ts` | `class-names.util.ts` |
| API mapper (DTO / shape transforms under `app/api/_shared/mappers/`) | `name-name.mapper.ts` | `user.mapper.ts` |
| Hook module | `name-name.hook.ts` | `use-media-query.hook.ts` |

Use **`.tsx`** for React component files that contain JSX (not `.ts`). Apply the same idea for other single-purpose modules when adding new roles. **Do not** use a bare `name-name.ts` for new files when one of the roles above fits.

## TanStack Query (`@tanstack/react-query`)

- **Provider:** **`QueryClientProviderComponent`** in **`src/features/query/providers/query-client-provider.component.tsx`**. Root layout nests **`AuthProvider`** inside it (see [ARCHITECTURE.md](../../ARCHITECTURE.md) — **TanStack Query**).
- **Hooks:** Add **`useQuery`** / **`useMutation`** wrappers under **`src/hooks/api/<domain>/`** as **`*.hook.ts`** (e.g. **`use-user-profile.query.hook.ts`**, **`use-save-profile.mutation.hook.ts`**). Consumers must be **client** components.
- **Query keys:** Colocate **`*.query-keys.const.ts`** factories in the same **`src/hooks/api/<domain>/`** folder; use them for stable **`queryKey`** arrays.
- **Same standards:** Arrow functions only, no **`any`**, multi-arg helpers use **`I<FuncName>Props`**.

## Arrow functions — no `function` keyword

Do **not** declare functions with the `function` keyword. Use **`const name = …`** with an **arrow function** for module-level helpers, exports, and nested functions. **Class methods** use the same style as **arrow instance fields** (e.g. `update = ({ id, patch }: IUpdateProps) => { … }`) so the `function` keyword is not used.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
function parse(input: string): number {
  return Number(input);
}

// ✅ GOOD
const parse = (input: string): number => Number(input);

// ✅ GOOD — exported async
export const loadUser = async (id: string): Promise<IUser | null> => {
  // ...
};
```

## App Router API — service, repository, and mapper exports

For modules under **`app/api/_shared/services/`**, **`app/api/_shared/repository/`**, **`app/api/_shared/mappers/`**, and **`app/api/**/service/`** (provider-specific), export **one object** per file as the main callable API. Use **`camelCase`** names: `authService`, `tryCatchService`, `userRepository`, `userMapper`, `googleOAuthService`. Callers import that object and invoke methods (e.g. `authService.buildOauthRedirect(props)`, `userMapper.toUserMe(user)`).

**Mappers** are **pure transforms** between persistence/schema types, **`app/api/_shared/interfaces`**, and **`src/interfaces`** DTOs — no DB I/O. One file per concern (e.g. **`user.mapper.ts`** → **`userMapper`**); add **`<entity>.mapper.ts`** when another entity needs mapping.

- Keep **types** (`interface` / shared props) exported from the same file when they are API-layer only; promote to `src/interfaces` when shared more broadly (see [ARCHITECTURE.md](../../ARCHITECTURE.md) §5).
- **Do not** rely on many separate exported function names as the primary pattern for these layers — the object groups the module’s public surface.
- **Private helpers (service / repository / mapper modules):** A **private** helper is a module-level `const` that does **not** appear as a key on **`export const authService` / `tryCatchService` / `userRepository` / `userMapper` / `googleOAuthService` / …**. Mark those **only** with:
  ```ts
  /**
   * PRIVATE
   */
  const helperName = ...
  ```
- **Do not** add other `/** … */` comment blocks on implementation `const`s in those files — no descriptive JSDoc on methods that **are** listed on the exported object, and no extra docs on the public `const` exports themselves unless the team later chooses otherwise.

```typescript
const buildOauthRedirect = ({
  request,
  path,
  query,
  stateCookieName,
}: IBuildOauthRedirectProps): NextResponse => {
  // ...
};

export const authService = {
  clearOAuthStateCookie,
  buildOauthRedirect,
};
```

```typescript
import { authService } from "@/app/api/_shared/services/auth/auth.service";

authService.buildOauthRedirect({ request, path, query, stateCookieName });
```

```typescript
import { userMapper } from "@/app/api/_shared/mappers/user.mapper";

userMapper.toAppUser(doc);
userMapper.toUserMe(appUser);
```

**`tryCatchService`** ([`app/api/_shared/services/try-catch/try-catch.service.ts`](../../app/api/_shared/services/try-catch/try-catch.service.ts)): shared **`runSync`** / **`runAsync`** wrappers that return **`null`** on any throw so callers can use **`const`** + null checks instead of `let` + try/catch assignment. Use for API orchestration when errors are handled uniformly (e.g. redirect with a code). Do **not** use when you need **`catch (error)`** for logging or typed error handling — keep an explicit `try` / `catch` there.

```typescript
import { tryCatchService } from "@/app/api/_shared/services/try-catch/try-catch.service";

const env = tryCatchService.runSync(() => readGoogleOauthEnv());
if (env === null) {
  return /* error response */;
}

const data = await tryCatchService.runAsync(() => fetchSomething());
if (data === null) {
  return /* error response */;
}
```

Placement and folder layout: [ARCHITECTURE.md](../../ARCHITECTURE.md) §5. Step-by-step skill for agents: [.cursor/skills/api-app-service/SKILL.md](../../.cursor/skills/api-app-service/SKILL.md).

## Component map — existence and reuse

Before implementing **new** UI (a new component file or a one-off that could become shared), open [COMPONENT_MAP.md](../../COMPONENT_MAP.md) at the repository root.

- **Check** whether something already exists under the listed paths (`components/ui/`, `src/components/`, `app/.../components/`, route layouts/pages).
- **Prefer reuse:** import and compose or extend an existing component when it fits; avoid near-duplicates.
- **Keep the map current:** when you add, rename, move, or remove a component that belongs in the catalog, update `COMPONENT_MAP.md` in the **same** change (see ARCHITECTURE.md).

## No `any`

Do not use `any`. Prefer explicit types, generics, or `unknown` with narrowing.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
const parseBad = (input: any): number => Number(input);

// ✅ GOOD — explicit type
const parse = (input: string | number): number => Number(input);

// ✅ GOOD — unknown + narrowing
const parseUnknown = (input: unknown): number => {
  if (typeof input === "string" || typeof input === "number") {
    return Number(input);
  }
  throw new TypeError("Expected string or number");
};
```

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
const data: any = JSON.parse(raw);

// ✅ GOOD
interface ParsedShape {
  id: string;
  count: number;
}
const data = JSON.parse(raw) as ParsedShape;
// or validate with a schema library and typed result
```

## Multi-argument functions → `I<FuncName>Props`

If a function (or method) has **more than one** parameter, replace positional parameters with a **single object** whose type is an interface named **`I` + `PascalCase` function name + `Props`**.

**Destructure that object in the parameter list** (`({ name, email, role }: ICreateUserProps) => …`). Do **not** take a single `props` parameter and then assign `const { … } = props` on the next line unless you need the whole object (rare).

Single-parameter functions stay as-is (no props interface required).

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — more than one positional argument
const createUserBad = (
  name: string,
  email: string,
  role: "admin" | "user"
): void => {
  // ...
};

// ✅ GOOD — one object + ICreateUserProps, destructure in the signature
interface ICreateUserProps {
  name: string;
  email: string;
  role: "admin" | "user";
}

const createUser = ({
  name,
  email,
  role,
}: ICreateUserProps): void => {
  // ...
};

// ❌ BAD — redundant props + destructure
const createUserVerbose = (props: ICreateUserProps): void => {
  const { name, email, role } = props;
};
```

```typescript
// ✅ OK — only one argument, no I*Props needed
const getUserById = (id: string): void => {
  // ...
};
```

For **class methods**, use the class method name in the interface: e.g. `save` with two args → `ISaveProps` (or `IUserRepositorySaveProps` if you need disambiguation across classes).

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
class UserRepositoryBad {
  update(id: string, patch: Partial<User>): void {}
}

// ✅ GOOD
interface IUpdateProps {
  id: string;
  patch: Partial<User>;
}

class UserRepository {
  update = ({ id, patch }: IUpdateProps): void => {
    // ...
  };
}
```

## React components — props and local types

Use **`interface`** for object shapes in components. Name them with an **`I` prefix** (`IUser`, `IProps`, …). Do not rely on implicit `any` for props.

### Local shapes (same file only)

If a shape is used only inside one component file, define it **above** the component in that file.

```tsx
interface IUser {
  id: string;
  name: string;
}

export const UserCard = () => {
  const user: IUser = {
    id: "1",
    name: "John",
  };

  return <div>{user.name}</div>;
};
```

### Component props

Every component that accepts props must be **explicitly** typed. If the props shape is not reused elsewhere, use **`interface IProps`** in the same file.

```tsx
interface IProps {
  title: string;
  onClick: () => void;
}

export const Button = ({ title, onClick }: IProps) => (
  <button onClick={onClick}>{title}</button>
);
```

**Rule of thumb:** shape used only here → same-file `interface` with `I` prefix (`IProps` for props, `IUser` for a local entity, etc.). When an object shape is shared across modules, extract it to `src/interfaces`; when a shared **`type`** alias or union/utility type is needed, use `src/types` (see `ARCHITECTURE.md`) — avoid over-engineering until then.

**Note:** Plain functions with several parameters still use **`I` + function name + `Props`** (e.g. `ICreateUserProps` above), not `IProps`. **`IProps`** is for **React component** props colocated in the component file.
